{
	title: 'Plugin tools get great cards by declaration',
	spec: "A plugin tool declares its card on the Tool definition (host/tools.ts ToolCard): optional card { command?: input field holding a raw command, language?: its syntax, hue?: OKLCH hue, icon?: web icon name, title?(input, result?): what the call works on }. Any tool without a built-in module gets a card (host/tool-card.ts). readOnly stays the effect kind: read-only tools run in parallel and default to READ's green; others default to BASH's purple; a hue, set only when the user asks, overrides the color but never the kind. Title: the tool's name as built-in titles show it (sentence case, like Google), a space, then the call purpose (/* comment */ or description) or else card.title; never the command. Second row: the raw command as code, also on a closed terminal card; other arguments as key: value text; then the result in full, a failure once. Purpose comments are kept and taught in HELP for every carded tool. Both clients color the card from its hue at the shared fgL/fgC/bgL/bgC levels (colors.cardHue; the web has a .tool-hue-N class per 5 degrees), and the web shows the icon, default sparkle. The look rides on the recorded presentation, so history replays it without the plugin. skills/plugin-tools teaches tool plugins: the one-line tools.all hook, card fields, kind, color range, icons and four worked examples; the system prompt skill list names it. example-plugin.ts shows a card tool.",
